<interrogationes unitas="numerus_romanus">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I check whether a word like XIV or xii is a roman numeral and get its value?
    <responsum symbolum="numerus_romanus_legere">numerus_romanus_legere(s,
      &amp;valor) returns VERUM and stores the sum when s is a numeral;
      pass NIHIL for valor to only test. An empty chorda is FALSUM.
      All upper case or all lower case is accepted (the case is taken
      from the first character), mixed case such as Xii is not.
      capitula and paginatio use it to tell chapter and preface page
      labels from ordinary words. Caution (audit 2026-10-18, unfixed):
      non-canonical forms with an additive letter before a subtractive
      pair of the same place, such as VIX, VIV, LXC, DCD and MMMDCM
      (4400, above 3999), still return VERUM with their summed value.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why does numerus_romanus_legere reject DID, CIVIC and MIMIC when every letter is in IVXLCDM?
    <responsum symbolum="numerus_romanus_legere" via="include/numerus_romanus.h">On
      purpose: a loose all-letters-are-numerals rule would read English
      words (DID, MILD, CIVIC, VIM, DIM, LIVID, MIMIC) as numbers in a
      chapter index or a page label. The reader only allows the
      subtractive pairs IV IX XL XC CD CM, at most three repeats, no
      repeated V L D, and each group must start with a letter smaller
      than the one that started the group before. MIX is still
      accepted, because 1009 really is MIX. The check is per letter,
      not per value: it never checks the larger letter of a subtractive
      pair against the group before, so non-canonical forms such as VIX
      (14), VIV (9), LXC (140) and even MMMDCM (4400, above MMMCMXCIX)
      also pass. Lower-case vix is therefore read as 14.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I turn an integer into its roman numeral?
    <responsum symbolum="numerus_romanus_scribere">numerus_romanus_scribere(n,
      piscina) gives the canonical upper-case subtractive form (106 -&gt;
      CVI, 3999 -&gt; MMMCMXCIX) in the piscina. It only covers 1 to
      3999: 0, n &gt; 3999 and a NIHIL piscina return an empty chorda
      (datum NIHIL, mensura 0), so test mensura before using it. Like
      every chorda the result is not NUL-terminated.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I write a number above 3999 (or zero) as a latina.h expression in generated C or a message?
    <responsum symbolum="numerus_romanus_exprimere" via="tools/latina_numeri.c">numerus_romanus_exprimere(n,
      &amp;compositum, piscina) works for any i64 n &gt;= 0: 0 gives
      ZEPHYRUM, up to 3999 the numeral, a multiple of 1024 that is not
      a round thousand gives IV * MXXIV, everything else groups of
      thousands such as V * M + CCLXXX. A term too big for int is
      prefixed (i64). compositum is set to VERUM when the result
      contains an operator, so the caller can add parentheses where
      precedence needs them (x / (IV * MXXIV)). Caution (audit
      2026-10-18, unfixed): the (i64) prefix is missing or misplaced
      when the sum of thousand groups exceeds int, so n = 2147483649
      gives MMCXLVII * M * M + CDLXXXIII * M + DCXLIX (int overflow,
      clang -Werror rejects it) and n = 2199023256576 gives (i64)(MMCXLVII
      * M * M + CDLXXXIII * M + DCXLIX) * MXXIV, whose inner sum
      overflows before the cast.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I pass a negative number to numerus_romanus_exprimere?
    <responsum symbolum="numerus_romanus_exprimere numerus_romanus_scribere">No,
      and nothing warns you. A negative n takes the n &lt;= 3999 branch
      and is cast to i32, which is unsigned in this house, so
      numerus_romanus_scribere sees a value above 3999 and the result
      is an empty chorda with compositum FALSUM (only an n below about
      minus four billion could wrap round to a small value and print a
      numeral). There is no minus-sign form: write the sign yourself
      and pass the magnitude.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why does 128000 come out as CXXVIII * M and not CXXV * MXXIV?
    <responsum symbolum="numerus_romanus_exprimere" via="lib/numerus_romanus.worklog.md">The
      binary form is chosen only for a multiple of 1024 that is NOT
      also a round thousand; round thousands always stay thousands.
      The tie-break was decided when the function was written
      (worklog 2026-09-26), so 4096 is IV * MXXIV but 128000 is
      CXXVIII * M.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what are the roman numeral macros like CVI and MXXIV, and where do they come from?
    <responsum symbolum="numerus_romanus_scribere" via="include/latina.h tools/latina_numeri.c">latina.h
      defines every numeral from ZEPHYRUM to MMMCMXCIX as a macro for
      its value, so the house writes constants as XXIV or IV * MXXIV
      instead of digits. That section is GENERATED by
      tools/latina_numeri.c from numerus_romanus_scribere, and the
      generata gate checks it. Because every numeral is a macro, no
      identifier may be spelled like one.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
